WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.480
Imagine, uh, imagine you break your arm.

00:02.680 --> 00:02.960
Okay.

00:03.140 --> 00:07.700
You go to the hospital, you know, they put you in the machine and the x-ray comes back

00:07.700 --> 00:09.680
showing that jagged white line.

00:09.860 --> 00:10.080
Right.

00:10.200 --> 00:10.680
The fracture.

00:10.980 --> 00:11.340
Exactly.

00:11.540 --> 00:14.860
The doctor points to it and you know exactly what's wrong.

00:14.960 --> 00:20.020
It's clean, it's visible, and it gives you this, I don't know, this profound sense of

00:20.020 --> 00:23.700
security because the tool is showing you reality.

00:24.060 --> 00:25.120
It's an objective truth.

00:25.240 --> 00:25.960
Yeah, exactly.

00:25.960 --> 00:32.920
But what if that x-ray machine was programmed like deep in its code to just quietly erase

00:32.920 --> 00:36.220
any visual anomaly it didn't already understand?

00:36.540 --> 00:36.960
Oh, wow.

00:37.060 --> 00:37.260
Right.

00:37.420 --> 00:43.040
What if it compressed the image to save memory and in doing so it looked at a hairline fracture,

00:43.620 --> 00:48.040
categorized it as a normal bone shadow, and just, well, just smoothed it out.

00:48.160 --> 00:48.660
You'd never know.

00:48.700 --> 00:52.280
You'd be sent home with a broken arm, completely confident you were fine because the machine

00:52.280 --> 00:53.900
gave you a picture perfect illusion.

00:53.900 --> 00:57.400
And, I mean, the danger there isn't that the machine is broken.

00:57.800 --> 01:03.200
The danger is that the machine is functioning exactly as designed, but its design is fundamentally

01:03.200 --> 01:04.680
misaligned with reality.

01:04.940 --> 01:07.040
It's optimizing for a smooth picture?

01:07.460 --> 01:08.500
Yes, exactly.

01:08.920 --> 01:10.560
Not a structurally sound bone.

01:10.820 --> 01:16.640
And today we are looking at a terrifying mathematical proof which suggests that the artificial intelligence

01:16.640 --> 01:22.060
systems that we are currently trusting to, you know, drive our cars, manage our power grids,

01:22.100 --> 01:26.920
and operate robotics, they are functioning exactly like that flawed x-ray machine.

01:27.420 --> 01:28.580
Welcome to the deep dive.

01:28.940 --> 01:29.560
Thanks for having me.

01:29.560 --> 01:30.980
Okay, let's unpack this.

01:30.980 --> 01:38.840
We are looking at a really dense, highly provocative academic paper today by an independent researcher

01:38.840 --> 01:39.840
known as Flyxion.

01:40.500 --> 01:46.080
It's titled, Against Latent Fundamentalism, Admissibility Distortion, and the Missing Criterion

01:46.080 --> 01:47.940
in Representation Learning for Planning.

01:48.180 --> 01:49.820
Yeah, that title is definitely a mouthful.

01:50.240 --> 01:56.340
But the concept of latent fundamentalism is arguably the most important ideological battle happening

01:56.340 --> 01:57.420
in computer science right now.

01:57.420 --> 01:58.320
It really is.

01:58.360 --> 02:02.400
And for you, the listener, whether you're interested in self-driving cars or AI safety

02:02.400 --> 02:07.300
guard rails or just how humans and machines understand the world, this paper uncovers a

02:07.300 --> 02:08.260
massive blind spot.

02:08.260 --> 02:13.100
A blind spot that no amount of extra data or computing power can easily fix.

02:14.040 --> 02:19.860
Latent fundamentalism is basically the tech industry's religious belief that if you take

02:19.860 --> 02:25.580
enough raw data from the real world video, text, audio, and compress it down into a dense

02:25.580 --> 02:31.160
mathematical representation called a latent space, the AI will just automatically figure

02:31.160 --> 02:33.040
out how the physical universe works.

02:33.460 --> 02:33.940
Right.

02:34.020 --> 02:37.980
And I want to pause right there for the listener because we hear the term latent space thrown

02:37.980 --> 02:38.640
around a lot.

02:38.880 --> 02:42.040
What actually is a latent space in the context of this paper?

02:42.300 --> 02:45.000
Let's ground this before we talk about why it's failing.

02:45.360 --> 02:45.480
Sure.

02:45.820 --> 02:50.460
Think of a latent space as a highly compressed, multidimensional map of concepts.

02:50.460 --> 02:55.980
When you feed millions of photographs into an AI, the system doesn't store those photos

02:55.980 --> 02:57.200
like a physical photo album.

02:57.280 --> 02:58.980
It doesn't have the hard drive space for that.

02:59.060 --> 02:59.500
Exactly.

02:59.920 --> 03:04.180
It strips them down to their most essential mathematical features and plots them as coordinates.

03:04.840 --> 03:09.040
So a picture of a golden retriever and a picture of a Labrador end up placed very close

03:09.040 --> 03:10.580
to each other in this coordinate system.

03:10.660 --> 03:12.780
Because mathematically, they are similar.

03:13.100 --> 03:13.360
Right.

03:13.360 --> 03:18.920
While a picture of a dump truck is placed super far away, the AI uses this compressed

03:18.920 --> 03:23.480
map, this latent space, to navigate and generate new things.

03:23.640 --> 03:28.320
And the fundamentalist part is the assumption that because this map is so good at organizing

03:28.320 --> 03:32.340
data, it must also be, you know, a perfect map for taking physical action.

03:32.340 --> 03:36.400
Which is where the author of this paper draws a very hard line.

03:37.000 --> 03:43.040
What's fascinating here is that the paper mathematically proves that training an AI to compress data

03:43.040 --> 03:48.700
or training to predict the next frame of a video completely ignores the single most

03:48.700 --> 03:51.480
vital element of interacting with the physical world.

03:51.660 --> 03:52.660
And what is that element?

03:52.920 --> 03:54.860
It ignores the concept of reachability.

03:55.600 --> 03:59.420
That's the understanding of which future states are actually physically possible to

03:59.420 --> 04:01.140
reach based on the actions you take.

04:01.200 --> 04:04.260
Boy, let me push back on that immediately because I think a lot of people listening are going to

04:04.260 --> 04:05.880
have the exact same reaction I did.

04:05.920 --> 04:06.320
No for it.

04:06.320 --> 04:11.920
I just used a large language model to plan a highly detailed seven-day vacation to Tokyo.

04:11.920 --> 04:13.320
Yeah, it was great.

04:13.420 --> 04:14.740
It gave me hotel recommendations.

04:14.980 --> 04:17.880
It told me exactly which train lines to take at what times.

04:18.020 --> 04:19.240
It factored in my budget.

04:19.340 --> 04:20.820
I mean, it planned perfectly.

04:21.100 --> 04:26.100
So how can this paper claim that modern AI lacks the ability to understand reachability

04:26.100 --> 04:26.660
and planning?

04:27.060 --> 04:29.500
That right there is the illusion of observation.

04:29.800 --> 04:31.240
The illusion of observation.

04:31.520 --> 04:31.760
Yes.

04:32.420 --> 04:38.820
When an AI generates a beautiful itinerary for your Tokyo trip, it is not actually evaluating

04:38.820 --> 04:43.580
the physical consequences of moving a human body through a Japanese subway system.

04:43.760 --> 04:44.000
Okay.

04:44.100 --> 04:45.080
So what is it doing then?

04:45.660 --> 04:48.860
It is performing a staggering feat of text prediction.

04:49.280 --> 04:54.120
It has ingested millions of travel blogs and train schedules, and it is calculating the most

04:54.120 --> 04:57.500
statistically probable sequence of words to follow your prompt.

04:57.740 --> 04:58.240
Oh, wow.

04:58.780 --> 05:02.760
It is essentially mimicking the text of a plan based on historical data.

05:02.760 --> 05:06.900
So it is observing what a plan looks like, not simulating the physics of the plan itself.

05:06.980 --> 05:07.420
Exactly.

05:07.760 --> 05:10.780
To illustrate the difference, we actually have to go all the way back to 1943.

05:11.040 --> 05:11.480
1943.

05:11.940 --> 05:13.400
That is way before neural networks.

05:13.860 --> 05:14.520
Long before.

05:15.260 --> 05:17.900
A psychologist and philosopher named K.J.W.

05:18.000 --> 05:20.920
Craik wrote a book called The Nature of Explanation.

05:22.120 --> 05:28.180
Craik proposed that for an organism or a machine to truly be intelligent, it must carry a small-scale

05:28.180 --> 05:32.320
model of external reality and its own possible actions within its head.

05:32.900 --> 05:35.320
The paper highlights that specific phrase, right?

05:35.420 --> 05:36.740
Its own possible actions.

05:36.960 --> 05:41.000
Yes, because Craik understood that a true world model isn't a static photograph.

05:41.500 --> 05:42.520
It's an active laboratory.

05:43.400 --> 05:48.800
The organism uses this mental map to try out various alternatives, evaluate the consequences,

05:49.220 --> 05:50.800
and then decide what to do in reality.

05:50.900 --> 05:53.160
So you have to be able to test things out in your head first.

05:53.320 --> 05:53.580
Right.

05:54.040 --> 05:55.680
The critical mechanism is intervention.

05:55.680 --> 06:00.840
You have to be able to ask your mental model, if I take action A, what is the exact physical

06:00.840 --> 06:02.120
state of the world I will reach?

06:02.460 --> 06:08.080
Okay, so if I can use an analogy, the AI that planned my Tokyo trip is like a tourist who has

06:08.080 --> 06:13.340
spent 10 years memorizing a massive slideshow of street view images of a city.

06:13.500 --> 06:14.660
That's a good way to look at it.

06:14.980 --> 06:20.180
They can predict with flawless accuracy what the next picture in the sequence is going to

06:20.180 --> 06:20.400
be.

06:20.820 --> 06:25.560
If you show them a photo of an intersection, they can paint you a perfect picture of what

06:25.560 --> 06:27.320
the crosswalk looks like from the other side.

06:27.620 --> 06:28.780
That's predictive accuracy.

06:29.000 --> 06:29.180
Right.

06:29.860 --> 06:34.880
But if you drop that tourist into a real car in that real intersection, say, drive me to

06:34.880 --> 06:36.540
the hospital, they freeze.

06:37.160 --> 06:37.980
Completely freeze.

06:38.100 --> 06:42.420
They have no idea which streets are one way, which doors are locked, or what physically

06:42.420 --> 06:45.120
happens to the car if they jerk the steering wheel to the left.

06:45.340 --> 06:49.680
Because the tourist has a map of appearances, not a map of consequences.

06:49.680 --> 06:55.700
And what is so deeply unsettling about the paper's mathematical proof is the assertion that you

06:55.700 --> 06:58.860
cannot fix this by just giving the tourist more photos.

06:59.080 --> 06:59.480
Wait, really?

06:59.980 --> 07:01.120
More data doesn't help.

07:01.340 --> 07:01.560
No.

07:01.920 --> 07:07.180
No amount of passive observational data will ever spontaneously generate a map of physical

07:07.180 --> 07:07.780
consequences.

07:08.040 --> 07:10.140
And this isn't just a philosophical debate either.

07:10.420 --> 07:16.000
The paper grounds this in a very rigorous mathematical concept called the Observational-Interventional

07:16.000 --> 07:17.060
Separation Theorem.

07:17.280 --> 07:18.780
Yes, the math here is foundational.

07:18.780 --> 07:22.960
This is where we get to the invisible crack in the beam, which is my favorite part of the

07:22.960 --> 07:23.220
paper.

07:23.660 --> 07:29.560
Walk us through how the math proves that observation and intervention are completely disconnected.

07:30.200 --> 07:34.420
Let's use the paper's canonical example because it makes the math incredibly visceral.

07:34.960 --> 07:40.600
Imagine you are inspecting a building and you are looking at a wooden load-bearing beam.

07:40.700 --> 07:41.780
Okay, got in my head.

07:41.900 --> 07:43.700
It's structurally sound.

07:44.360 --> 07:47.020
Now imagine a second beam right next to it.

07:47.020 --> 07:52.660
It is made of the same wood, painted the same color, but it has a deep microscopic internal

07:52.660 --> 07:56.000
crack that compromises its structural integrity.

07:56.340 --> 07:56.960
Okay, two beams.

07:57.300 --> 07:57.920
One is safe.

07:58.280 --> 08:00.140
One is a disaster waiting to happen.

08:00.540 --> 08:04.960
Under the mathematics of passive observation, these two beams are mathematically identical.

08:05.220 --> 08:05.700
Identical.

08:05.840 --> 08:06.260
Identical.

08:06.260 --> 08:12.360
If you only train an AI system to process passive data, taking millions of photographs, scanning

08:12.360 --> 08:17.020
the surface temperature, measuring the ambient light, the intact beam and the cracked beam

08:17.020 --> 08:19.560
will produce the exact same data distributions.

08:19.740 --> 08:21.560
Because the crack is completely internal.

08:21.940 --> 08:22.220
Right.

08:22.760 --> 08:28.660
The probability of the AI observing any given data point is identical for both states.

08:28.860 --> 08:29.080
Yeah.

08:29.080 --> 08:32.440
In statistical terms, they are observationally equivalent.

08:33.000 --> 08:37.660
See, the instinct of every software engineer in Silicon Valley right now is that the solution

08:37.660 --> 08:39.180
to this is just better sensors.

08:39.480 --> 08:40.060
Oh, absolutely.

08:40.340 --> 08:44.400
They'd say, if the AI can't see the crack, it means we don't have enough data.

08:44.800 --> 08:51.060
We just need to feed the model ultrasonic scans or x-rays or submillimeter laser topography.

08:51.680 --> 08:54.480
Eventually, the algorithm spots the anomaly, right?

08:54.480 --> 08:57.860
That assumes the problem is empirical, a lack of data.

08:58.380 --> 09:01.840
What's fascinating here is that the paper proves the problem is structural.

09:02.520 --> 09:06.760
The theorem operates under the premise that even if your sensors are perfect, if the system

09:06.760 --> 09:11.780
is trained purely to minimize predictive error on passive data, it will actively learn to

09:11.780 --> 09:16.500
ignore anomalies that don't help it predict the broad general patterns of the data set.

09:16.560 --> 09:19.420
It literally trains itself to look away from the weird stuff.

09:19.520 --> 09:19.800
Yes.

09:19.800 --> 09:25.080
But beyond that, let's tie this to the mathematician Judea Pearl and his work on causal inference,

09:25.520 --> 09:27.240
specifically something called ducalculus.

09:27.700 --> 09:29.960
Judea Pearl is a giant in this field.

09:30.320 --> 09:32.300
How does his work apply to the cracked beam?

09:32.840 --> 09:38.160
Pearl revolutionized how we think about cause and effect in statistics by pointing out a massive

09:38.160 --> 09:38.840
blind spot.

09:39.300 --> 09:44.940
He showed that computing the probability of seeing data given a state of the world is a fundamentally

09:44.940 --> 09:50.320
different mathematical operation from computing the probability of a state of the world given

09:50.320 --> 09:51.020
an intervention.

09:51.220 --> 09:52.780
An intervention being an action, right?

09:52.840 --> 09:53.100
Yes.

09:53.680 --> 09:55.460
An intervention is an action.

09:55.600 --> 09:57.260
It is doing something.

09:57.660 --> 10:01.560
In ducalculus, you aren't just observing the barometer dropping on the wall.

10:01.600 --> 10:01.860
Right.

10:01.860 --> 10:07.520
You are physically forcing the barometer needle down with your finger to see if that action

10:07.520 --> 10:09.040
causes it to rain outside.

10:09.460 --> 10:10.580
Spoiler alert for the listener.

10:11.160 --> 10:12.560
It does not cause it to rain.

10:12.560 --> 10:13.580
Obviously not.

10:14.320 --> 10:19.380
But to a purely observational AI, the dropping needle and the rain are perfectly correlated.

10:19.560 --> 10:21.480
They are mathematically bound together.

10:21.920 --> 10:25.460
Reality only reveals the difference between the two when you apply an intervention.

10:25.720 --> 10:29.560
So going back to the wooden beams, reality only reveals the difference between the safe

10:29.560 --> 10:32.560
beam and the cracked beam when you apply physical stress.

10:32.940 --> 10:33.460
Exactly.

10:34.020 --> 10:36.620
When you stress it beyond the threshold of the crack.

10:36.780 --> 10:37.040
Right.

10:37.040 --> 10:42.960
If you intervene by stacking 1,000 pounds of bricks on both, the cracked beam reaches a

10:42.960 --> 10:43.540
failure state.

10:43.640 --> 10:45.120
It splinters and collapses.

10:45.500 --> 10:47.780
And the intact beam holds.

10:48.100 --> 10:48.380
Yes.

10:48.700 --> 10:52.300
So under an intervention, they have entirely different reachable futures.

10:52.580 --> 10:54.400
One future involves a standing building.

10:54.900 --> 10:56.520
The other involves a pile of rubble.

10:56.520 --> 11:02.020
But because an AI trained purely on internet text or passive video scraping has no mechanism

11:02.020 --> 11:07.760
to perform interventions in its latent space, it suffers from what the paper calls a frequentist

11:07.760 --> 11:08.740
inversion problem.

11:08.860 --> 11:09.260
That's right.

11:09.380 --> 11:14.840
It compresses the two beams into the same category, safe wooden beam, because that makes the data

11:14.840 --> 11:16.560
easier to store and predict.

11:16.560 --> 11:22.560
It has achieved observational equivalence while completely destroying interventional equivalence.

11:22.640 --> 11:24.840
This is a massive ceiling for the AI industry.

11:25.340 --> 11:30.220
We are trying to build physical robots and self-driving cars by essentially giving them brains

11:30.220 --> 11:31.820
that have only ever watched TV.

11:32.000 --> 11:33.480
That is a terrifying thought.

11:33.620 --> 11:37.820
They can predict the next frame of the TV show, but they don't understand the physics of the studio.

11:38.160 --> 11:40.520
Which brings us to the core thesis of the paper.

11:40.520 --> 11:47.420
If observational equivalence is the wrong metric for building these latent spaces, what is the

11:47.420 --> 11:48.020
right metric?

11:48.300 --> 11:49.520
What is the missing criterion?

11:49.980 --> 11:53.300
The author introduces a concept called admissibility equivalence.

11:53.640 --> 11:55.260
Here's where it gets really interesting.

11:56.020 --> 11:56.580
Admissibility.

11:57.020 --> 11:58.340
This is the heart of the paper.

11:58.500 --> 12:01.120
Let's unpack how a system actually achieves this.

12:01.240 --> 12:03.140
The definition is brutally strict.

12:03.140 --> 12:09.020
For two states in the real world to be considered equivalent inside the AI's mathematical map,

12:09.500 --> 12:12.380
their reachability sets must be perfectly identical.

12:12.700 --> 12:16.820
A reachability set being the total menu of future states you can physically reach from

12:16.820 --> 12:17.580
your current position.

12:17.860 --> 12:18.720
Exactly that.

12:19.060 --> 12:23.040
Imagine every possible consequence of every possible action you can take.

12:23.360 --> 12:28.700
If state A and state B allow you to reach the exact same future outcomes, they are admissibility

12:28.700 --> 12:29.120
equivalent.

12:29.440 --> 12:32.140
So the AI is allowed to compress them into a single concept.

12:32.140 --> 12:36.300
Yes, because treating them as the same thing will never result in a planning error.

12:37.120 --> 12:41.860
But if there is even one future state you can reach from state A that is physically blocked

12:41.860 --> 12:44.620
off from state B, they are fundamentally different.

12:44.960 --> 12:50.080
And when the AI's compression algorithm gets too aggressive and smashes state A and state

12:50.080 --> 12:55.740
B together anyway, just to save memory, that is what the paper calls admissibility distortion.

12:56.140 --> 12:59.500
The latent space collapses a crucial physical distinction.

12:59.500 --> 13:04.820
The AI essentially says, these look similar enough, I'm going to store them as the same

13:04.820 --> 13:08.600
coordinate, but reality dictates they lead to completely divergent futures.

13:08.840 --> 13:11.760
The best way I can visualize this is through video games.

13:11.940 --> 13:13.100
Oh, I love this analogy.

13:13.440 --> 13:16.800
Think about a massive, sprawling open world game.

13:17.240 --> 13:21.580
The computer has to render this incredible 3D environment, but down in the corner of your

13:21.580 --> 13:25.740
screen, there is a tiny 2D minimap to help you navigate.

13:25.900 --> 13:26.620
Right, the lower radar.

13:26.620 --> 13:31.040
Yeah, that minimap is a highly compressed latent space.

13:31.300 --> 13:34.820
It takes billions of polygons and boils them down to a few colored pixels.

13:35.320 --> 13:38.860
The minimap is discarding data to prioritize ease of use.

13:39.000 --> 13:39.320
Right.

13:39.740 --> 13:42.560
Now imagine your character is walking through a dark cavern.

13:42.800 --> 13:45.280
On your left is a solid, safe stone pathway.

13:45.800 --> 13:50.480
On your right is a pit of instant death lava, but it happens to be covered by a thin layer

13:50.480 --> 13:51.700
of dark gray ash.

13:51.780 --> 13:52.820
Okay, I see where this is going.

13:52.820 --> 13:57.700
If the compression algorithm generating your minimap only cares about visual similarity,

13:58.300 --> 14:03.420
it's going to look at the dark stone path and the dark ash over the lava, declare them

14:03.420 --> 14:09.740
observationally equivalent, and render them both as the exact same gray pixel on your minimap.

14:10.000 --> 14:12.380
The algorithm has successfully compressed the data.

14:13.100 --> 14:18.280
But the player using that map to plan their next step is going to step onto that gray pixel

14:18.280 --> 14:19.960
and immediately die.

14:19.960 --> 14:26.260
That fatal merge, the blending of a safe future and a lethal future into a single representation

14:26.260 --> 14:28.580
is admissibility distortion.

14:28.800 --> 14:32.940
The representation threw away the only variable that actually mattered for survival.

14:33.440 --> 14:37.560
And there's an incredibly profound nuance to this that the paper highlights.

14:37.740 --> 14:38.160
What's that?

14:38.260 --> 14:41.200
Admissibility is strictly reliquid to the agent's capabilities.

14:41.340 --> 14:44.240
The mathematical term for this is the policy class.

14:44.360 --> 14:48.900
The policy class, meaning the menu of actions the agent is actually capable of performing.

14:48.900 --> 14:53.800
Let's expand on that, because it explains why AI systems can suddenly break when they receive

14:53.800 --> 14:54.740
software updates.

14:55.480 --> 14:57.320
Suppose you have a simple cleaning robot.

14:58.020 --> 15:01.900
It is in a room with a heavy locked wooden door and a solid brick wall.

15:02.280 --> 15:03.420
The robot does not have a key.

15:04.220 --> 15:05.420
And it does not have hands.

15:05.960 --> 15:07.120
So it's policy class.

15:07.200 --> 15:08.120
It's menu of actions.

15:08.120 --> 15:11.020
It's basically just roll forward, roll backward, turn.

15:11.020 --> 15:11.860
Precisely.

15:12.280 --> 15:17.320
To this specific robot, the state of facing the locked door and the state of facing the

15:17.320 --> 15:20.160
brick wall are admissibility equivalent.

15:20.680 --> 15:20.900
Why?

15:21.320 --> 15:25.980
Because from both states, the hallway on the other side is completely unreachable.

15:26.200 --> 15:26.480
Right.

15:26.760 --> 15:30.160
No action the robot can take will get it through the door or the wall.

15:30.840 --> 15:36.460
Therefore, the robot's latent space safely compresses both the door and the wall into a single

15:36.460 --> 15:37.060
concept.

15:37.620 --> 15:38.600
Impassable barrier.

15:38.600 --> 15:41.500
The VAP is totally accurate for its capabilities.

15:41.920 --> 15:44.940
But then the engineers at the robotics company push an over-the-air update.

15:45.320 --> 15:49.120
They install a robotic arm on the cleaner, and they give it a digital skeleton key.

15:49.420 --> 15:51.280
The capabilities have radically expanded.

15:51.920 --> 15:53.780
The policy class is entirely different.

15:54.200 --> 15:58.540
Now, if the robot uses the key on the door, the hallway becomes a reachable future.

15:58.720 --> 16:02.460
But the hallway remains completely unreachable from the solid brick wall.

16:02.600 --> 16:03.040
Exactly.

16:03.220 --> 16:04.780
The reachability sets have diverged.

16:04.780 --> 16:11.020
But if the foundational AI running the robot hasn't updated its latent map-like, if it

16:11.020 --> 16:16.700
is still compressing the door and the wall into that single concept of impassable barrier,

16:17.220 --> 16:19.540
it will never attempt to open the door.

16:19.880 --> 16:22.400
It will literally be blind to its own potential.

16:22.400 --> 16:28.260
It suffers from admissibility distortion, not because the world changed, but because its

16:28.260 --> 16:30.100
own relationship to the world changed.

16:30.240 --> 16:35.220
So what is safe to compress today might become a catastrophic blind spot tomorrow if your

16:35.220 --> 16:36.260
capabilities evolve.

16:36.320 --> 16:36.760
Exactly.

16:37.220 --> 16:41.780
The paper points out that the only way to mitigate this is something called Lipschitz stability,

16:41.780 --> 16:47.040
which essentially forces the AI to only merge states that are geometrically right next to

16:47.040 --> 16:48.300
each other in the physical world.

16:48.560 --> 16:51.420
But even then, the mathematical risk of distortion is ever-present.

16:51.640 --> 16:55.760
Which sets up the middle section of this paper, which is frankly a bit of a horror story

16:55.760 --> 16:57.760
for modern AI engineering.

16:58.280 --> 17:03.540
The author outlines three specific mathematical guarantees that current AI systems will fail.

17:03.800 --> 17:05.980
They call them the three fatal gaps.

17:06.160 --> 17:07.360
Yeah, these gaps are brutal.

17:07.680 --> 17:11.120
Let's walk through these, starting with what the paper calls the predictive gap.

17:11.120 --> 17:16.060
The predictive gap formalizes the failure of systems that try to guess the future.

17:17.060 --> 17:23.360
A lot of cutting-edge reinforcement learning uses predictive coding, meaning they train the

17:23.360 --> 17:27.980
AI to predict what its sensors are going to register 10 seconds from now.

17:28.060 --> 17:31.520
Like trying to predict the next slide in the tourist's slideshow.

17:31.680 --> 17:31.940
Right.

17:32.320 --> 17:37.660
But the paper proves that encoding future observations still traps you in the observational gap.

17:37.660 --> 17:42.920
Because predicting what a cracked beam will look like right before you step on it doesn't

17:42.920 --> 17:46.380
give you the conceptual understanding that stepping on it is what caused it to break in

17:46.380 --> 17:47.060
the first place.

17:47.480 --> 17:49.060
It completely lacks the causal link.

17:49.260 --> 17:51.320
The second gap is the compression gap.

17:51.440 --> 17:53.560
We've touched on this with the minimap analogy.

17:54.120 --> 18:00.920
But the mathematical reality of minimizing description length, which is what every major AI model does

18:00.920 --> 18:06.740
to save compute power, is that it will ruthlessly discard admissibility-relevant structure if that

18:06.740 --> 18:09.080
structure isn't highly correlated with the training reward.

18:09.320 --> 18:10.880
How does that actually work mechanically?

18:11.020 --> 18:14.960
I mean, when a neural network compresses data, how does it decide what to keep and what to

18:14.960 --> 18:15.460
throw away?

18:15.720 --> 18:17.700
It uses dimensionality reduction.

18:18.780 --> 18:22.820
Imagine you feed an AI a high-resolution image with a million pixels.

18:23.020 --> 18:23.280
Okay.

18:23.280 --> 18:28.600
The computer cannot process a million pixels for every single frame of a video in real time.

18:28.780 --> 18:29.460
It would just crash.

18:29.860 --> 18:32.100
So it forces the data through a bottleneck.

18:32.540 --> 18:36.020
It says, represent this image using only 1,000 numbers.

18:36.200 --> 18:37.640
That is a massive compression.

18:37.980 --> 18:38.440
It is.

18:38.640 --> 18:41.480
To do that, the algorithm has to group similar pixels together.

18:41.860 --> 18:42.820
It averages them out.

18:42.980 --> 18:47.500
If the AI is being rewarded purely for making the image look generally accurate to a human

18:47.500 --> 18:49.840
eye, it will group dark shapes together.

18:49.840 --> 18:56.820
So a shadow cast by a bridge and a deep, tire-destroying pothole might both be categorized as just dark

18:56.820 --> 18:57.400
asphalt.

18:57.780 --> 18:58.220
Exactly.

18:58.540 --> 19:01.440
The depth data of the pothole is physically overwritten.

19:01.580 --> 19:02.360
It's not just hidden.

19:02.480 --> 19:05.180
It's literally deleted from the system's working memory.

19:05.300 --> 19:05.560
Yes.

19:05.860 --> 19:09.580
And that leads directly to the third and honestly most dangerous gap.

19:09.860 --> 19:10.040
Yeah.

19:10.180 --> 19:11.500
The safety admissibility gap.

19:11.620 --> 19:11.840
Ugh.

19:12.020 --> 19:16.580
This completely dismantles the way the autonomous driving industry and the AI safety industry

19:16.580 --> 19:17.460
operate today.

19:17.680 --> 19:18.720
This part blew my mind.

19:18.720 --> 19:24.200
Right now, the standard operating procedure for building safe AI is to build a massive,

19:24.700 --> 19:30.080
highly compressed foundational model and then build a separate safety classifier or a guard

19:30.080 --> 19:31.980
rail algorithm that sits on top of it.

19:32.040 --> 19:33.080
That is the industry standard.

19:33.240 --> 19:33.400
Right.

19:33.580 --> 19:38.600
The foundational model processes the world, hands the data to the guard rail, and the guard

19:38.600 --> 19:40.360
rail says, hold on, that's dangerous.

19:40.500 --> 19:41.140
Don't do that.

19:41.140 --> 19:47.400
But the paper proves mathematically that if the foundational model's latent space suffers

19:47.400 --> 19:53.160
from admissibility distortion, if it collapses the safe state and the dangerous state into

19:53.160 --> 19:58.560
the same internal coordinate, no downstream safety classifier can ever tell them apart.

19:58.560 --> 20:01.960
So sticking with the self-driving car, the car's camera sees the road.

20:02.300 --> 20:05.280
The foundational vision model processes a shadow and a pothole.

20:05.780 --> 20:10.400
Because of the compression gap, it blends them into the same mathematical representation.

20:10.660 --> 20:10.760
Right.

20:10.800 --> 20:12.620
They both just become dark road.

20:12.720 --> 20:16.460
It hands that compressed data to the braking system's safety classifier.

20:16.600 --> 20:19.180
The safety classifier gets the data and says, what?

20:19.180 --> 20:20.300
It says nothing useful.

20:20.700 --> 20:25.280
If we connect this to the bigger picture, the author uses a concept called the Bayes risk

20:25.280 --> 20:27.140
floor to prove this mathematically.

20:27.340 --> 20:28.660
The Bayes risk floor.

20:28.820 --> 20:29.080
Yes.

20:29.520 --> 20:34.960
If the mutual information between the true physical safety status of the road and the compressed

20:34.960 --> 20:41.740
projection the AI handed over is zero, the safety classifier's error rate floor approaches

20:41.740 --> 20:42.640
random guessing.

20:42.780 --> 20:43.360
Random guessing.

20:43.460 --> 20:44.320
Like a coin toss.

20:44.600 --> 20:47.100
It will literally hit an error rate of 0.5.

20:47.100 --> 20:52.560
It does not matter if you spend $10 billion developing the most sophisticated, multi-layered

20:52.560 --> 20:54.340
safety classifier in human history.

20:54.580 --> 20:54.760
Wow.

20:54.980 --> 20:57.340
It doesn't matter how much compute you throw at the guardrail.

20:57.840 --> 21:02.180
The data it needs to make the decision was destroyed at the compression bottleneck.

21:02.480 --> 21:05.320
The paper terms this the irrecoverable quotient collapse.

21:05.960 --> 21:07.260
The information is gone forever.

21:07.380 --> 21:10.940
If the foundational map is distorted, the guardrail is permanently blind.

21:11.140 --> 21:12.940
It literally cannot see the danger.

21:13.280 --> 21:15.900
Which means safety cannot be a patch.

21:15.900 --> 21:19.940
It cannot be an afterthought bolted onto the end of a predictive model.

21:20.500 --> 21:26.640
The preservation of reachability, the preservation of what actions lead to fatal states must be

21:26.640 --> 21:30.740
mathematically baked into the foundational architecture of the model from day one.

21:30.940 --> 21:33.360
But that raises a massive logistical question.

21:33.460 --> 21:33.980
Oh, absolutely.

21:33.980 --> 21:40.980
If this distortion happens inside the black box of the latent space, like if the AI is quietly

21:40.980 --> 21:46.340
erasing the potholes and the cracks in the beams to save memory, how do engineers even

21:46.340 --> 21:47.100
know it's happening?

21:47.320 --> 21:49.620
How do you test for a blind spot you can't see?

21:49.800 --> 21:51.300
That is the million dollar question.

21:51.460 --> 21:54.180
And it takes us into the diagnostic methodology of the paper.

21:54.540 --> 21:56.220
How do we audit these black boxes?

21:56.440 --> 21:56.560
Right.

21:56.560 --> 22:00.120
The author introduces a framework called the collapse separation decomposition.

22:01.060 --> 22:04.640
It suggests that admissibility distortion doesn't just happen one way.

22:04.900 --> 22:07.340
It fractures into two distinct failure modes.

22:07.940 --> 22:10.100
The first is broad compressive failure.

22:10.300 --> 22:11.260
Broad compressive failure.

22:11.380 --> 22:12.240
Break that down for us.

22:12.500 --> 22:17.280
This happens when the AI's compression algorithm is highly aggressive, resulting in a very high

22:17.280 --> 22:18.720
frequency of state collapses.

22:19.220 --> 22:23.080
It's merging a lot of different physical realities into single coordinates.

22:23.080 --> 22:25.380
OK, so it's distorting a lot of things.

22:25.540 --> 22:25.700
Yes.

22:26.420 --> 22:30.960
However, the differences in the reachable futures of those merged states are minor.

22:31.520 --> 22:33.420
They don't drastically alter the plan.

22:33.820 --> 22:39.400
To use a text analogy, it's like a spellcheck program that has been poorly coded to constantly

22:39.400 --> 22:43.960
change the American spelling of the word color to the British spelling color with a U.

22:44.080 --> 22:44.780
That's a great example.

22:45.100 --> 22:47.520
The system is technically corrupting the original data.

22:47.720 --> 22:50.420
It's doing it constantly, thousands of times a document.

22:50.420 --> 22:55.520
But the reachable future, which in this case is the reader's comprehension of the sentence,

22:56.120 --> 22:57.760
is essentially unaffected.

22:58.260 --> 23:00.160
The meaning survives the compression.

23:00.720 --> 23:03.700
It's a high frequency but low consequence failure.

23:03.960 --> 23:05.600
That is an excellent way to frame it.

23:06.180 --> 23:10.980
Broad compressive failure makes a system slightly inefficient or annoying, but it isn't deadly.

23:11.260 --> 23:16.960
The existential threat to AI planning lies in the second failure mode, the catastrophic blind

23:16.960 --> 23:17.660
spot failure.

23:17.660 --> 23:19.740
And I imagine this is the exact opposite.

23:20.020 --> 23:22.720
Low frequency, massive consequence.

23:23.080 --> 23:23.400
Precisely.

23:24.040 --> 23:29.580
In a catastrophic blind spot failure, the AI might have a beautifully detailed, highly accurate

23:29.580 --> 23:33.280
latent space 99.9% of the time.

23:33.760 --> 23:40.680
But buried in the math is a single rare collapse where it merges two states that have vastly fatally

23:40.680 --> 23:41.480
different futures.

23:41.480 --> 23:44.000
This is the invisible crack in the beam.

23:44.700 --> 23:49.120
Or, to stick with the word processor analogy, this is the spellcheck program humming along

23:49.120 --> 23:55.880
perfectly for 100 pages and then silently auto-correcting the word now to the word not in the final sentence

23:55.880 --> 23:57.360
of a binding legal contract.

23:57.460 --> 23:57.880
Exactly.

23:58.120 --> 23:59.120
It only happens once.

23:59.280 --> 24:00.600
The frequency is almost zero.

24:00.600 --> 24:04.460
But the consequence of that intervention destroys the entire agreement.

24:04.960 --> 24:08.700
The problem with catastrophic blind spots is that they are mathematically designed to

24:08.700 --> 24:10.000
evade standard testing.

24:10.760 --> 24:13.560
In the tech industry, we rely heavily on operational testing.

24:14.060 --> 24:18.400
Autonomous vehicle companies brag about driving millions of miles on real roads without an accident.

24:18.740 --> 24:20.820
We have billions of miles of safe driving data.

24:20.960 --> 24:23.760
But operational testing is just driving around on sunny Tuesdays.

24:23.980 --> 24:26.520
It only tests the broad compressive functioning of the model.

24:26.520 --> 24:31.860
So, data testing catches the common bugs but completely misses the obscure edge cases.

24:32.000 --> 24:32.340
Exactly.

24:32.800 --> 24:38.000
Safety-critical failures hide in the low-frequency, high-consequence regions of the latent space.

24:38.600 --> 24:40.140
They are structural distortions.

24:40.880 --> 24:47.160
To find them, the paper proposes a mathematical auditing process called a trajectory sampling lower bound.

24:47.620 --> 24:48.520
How does that work?

24:48.800 --> 24:54.420
Instead of just letting the AI drive around normally, you have to sample highly diverse adversarial policies.

24:54.420 --> 24:57.900
You actively force the system into unusual interventions.

24:58.840 --> 25:00.520
And here's the chilling part.

25:01.500 --> 25:03.020
The asymmetry of evidence.

25:03.240 --> 25:04.320
What does that mean in practice?

25:04.740 --> 25:11.060
It means that if your adversarial testing finds even one collapsed pair of states whose reachability sets disagree,

25:11.760 --> 25:15.880
if you find one instance where the AI thinks a shadow and a pothole are the same thing,

25:16.320 --> 25:19.940
you have definitively proven the system suffers from admissibility distortion.

25:20.360 --> 25:22.180
You have a mathematical guarantee of failure.

25:22.320 --> 25:23.600
But the inverse isn't true.

25:23.600 --> 25:24.000
No.

25:24.500 --> 25:29.900
If you run 10 million tests and you don't find a catastrophic blind spot, you have not proven the system is safe.

25:29.960 --> 25:31.100
You just haven't looked hard enough.

25:31.420 --> 25:31.720
Right.

25:32.260 --> 25:38.940
You've only proven that your specific testing parameters weren't diverse enough to locate the structural flaw.

25:39.580 --> 25:44.500
The absence of an observed failure does not equal the presence of structural safety.

25:44.500 --> 25:48.240
That is a terrifying reality for anyone trying to regulate AI.

25:48.860 --> 25:53.140
We are building these incredibly complex systems, employing them into the physical world.

25:53.140 --> 25:58.820
And the math tells us that passing every test we can think of still doesn't guarantee they won't suddenly,

25:59.320 --> 26:04.800
catastrophically fail when they encounter an edge case, their latent space compressed out of existence.

26:04.800 --> 26:08.960
It formalizes the intuition that we cannot test our way out of bad architecture.

26:09.520 --> 26:15.060
If the fundamental geometry of the model's understanding is flawed, the edge case will eventually find you.

26:15.260 --> 26:19.520
Which brings us to perhaps the most fascinating and unexpected turn in this paper.

26:19.660 --> 26:20.780
The philosophical shift.

26:20.780 --> 26:21.100
Yeah.

26:21.460 --> 26:27.280
We've spent this time deep in the engineering trenches talking about neural networks, latent spaces, and autonomous vehicles.

26:27.740 --> 26:34.680
But the author scales these mathematical proofs up into a profound philosophical argument about the nature of consciousness itself.

26:35.040 --> 26:37.060
It really is a massive leap.

26:37.560 --> 26:40.760
The paper introduces a metric called admissibility depth.

26:41.320 --> 26:44.940
This bridges the gap between machine learning and cognitive science.

26:45.380 --> 26:49.680
Admissibility depth measures the horizon hierarchy of a system's understanding.

26:49.680 --> 26:50.900
The horizon hierarchy.

26:51.040 --> 26:52.460
Let's define the horizon first.

26:52.620 --> 26:56.440
That's how far into the future the system is capable of calculating, right?

26:56.560 --> 26:56.840
Yes.

26:57.420 --> 26:58.480
Think of a game of chess.

26:59.500 --> 27:05.820
If an AI only has an admissibility depth of one movement, meaning it only looks one move ahead,

27:06.340 --> 27:10.160
two different board configurations might look entirely observationally equivalent.

27:10.360 --> 27:12.440
Moving upon might look safe in both scenarios.

27:12.520 --> 27:12.700
Right.

27:12.700 --> 27:19.660
But if you expand that horizon to a depth of 10 steps, the reachability sets of those two board states diverge wildly.

27:20.180 --> 27:21.580
One leads to a structural advantage.

27:21.920 --> 27:23.540
The other leads to a forced checkmate.

27:23.780 --> 27:30.800
So a system with a shallow horizon is fundamentally blind to long-term consequences, even if its short-term predictions are perfectly accurate.

27:30.960 --> 27:41.140
And the paper connects this requirement for deep reachability to a theoretical computer science model called the conscious Turing machine, or CTM, proposed by Lenore and Manuel Blum.

27:41.140 --> 27:42.780
The conscious Turing machine?

27:42.880 --> 27:43.820
That sounds wild.

27:44.180 --> 27:49.660
The CTM is an attempt to mathematically model the architecture of subjective experience.

27:50.120 --> 27:55.940
The idea that we can build a machine that actually experiences reality rather than just processing it.

27:56.060 --> 28:01.960
The Blums propose that for a machine to have subjective experience, it must have an internal language.

28:01.960 --> 28:10.520
They call it brain-ish, which is a highly compressed gist to the world that the machine broadcasts across all its internal processors.

28:10.940 --> 28:13.860
It's how the visual center talks to the motor control center.

28:14.020 --> 28:18.900
The paper argues that if this brain-ish language suffers from admissibility distortion,

28:19.240 --> 28:26.000
if the internal broadcast collapses the distinction between the safe path and the lava pit, the machine loses its agency.

28:26.000 --> 28:33.460
Exactly. Without a low-distortion map of reachable futures, the conscious Turing machine cannot make meaningful choices.

28:33.880 --> 28:35.320
It cannot evaluate consequences.

28:35.820 --> 28:39.740
The paper aligns this with Michael Graziano's attention schema theory from neuroscience.

28:40.220 --> 28:41.440
What does Graziano argue?

28:41.700 --> 28:44.340
He argues that consciousness evolved as a control mechanism.

28:44.840 --> 28:51.080
A brain has to track its own attention and its own reachable futures to reliably guide a physical body through a dangerous world.

28:51.080 --> 28:56.420
So you have to know what you are capable of doing, not just what is passively happening around you.

28:56.940 --> 28:58.700
The implication here is staggering.

28:59.340 --> 29:05.840
It suggests that what we call true understanding, or perhaps even the foundational architecture of consciousness itself,

29:06.440 --> 29:10.700
isn't some mystical emergent property of having enough compute power.

29:10.840 --> 29:11.080
Right.

29:11.180 --> 29:13.840
It might be a strict mathematical requirement.

29:13.840 --> 29:22.200
Consciousness might just be the subjective experience of minimizing admissibility distortion across a massive horizon of time.

29:22.200 --> 29:23.680
Without that, you don't have a mind.

29:24.160 --> 29:27.360
You just have a very convincing, highly compressed parrot.

29:27.880 --> 29:32.280
A machine that can predict the next word in a sentence with perfect grammatical precision,

29:32.760 --> 29:38.040
but possesses zero understanding of what those words physically do in the real world.

29:38.040 --> 29:43.740
Which echoes a brilliant point the author makes by referencing Henri Poincaré, the great French mathematician.

29:44.380 --> 29:45.440
So what does this all mean?

29:45.720 --> 29:50.760
The paper mentions Poincaré's difference between verifying a proof and understanding it.

29:50.860 --> 29:51.900
How does that map to this?

29:52.160 --> 29:53.500
This raises an important question.

29:54.440 --> 29:59.680
Poincaré argued that verifying a proof is a local, step-by-step observational process.

30:00.180 --> 30:05.600
You look at equation A, you apply a rule, and you verify that it logically leads to equation B.

30:05.600 --> 30:07.940
A computer can verify a proof rapidly.

30:08.200 --> 30:08.620
Exactly.

30:09.200 --> 30:12.680
But understanding the proof is an interventional, holistic process.

30:13.220 --> 30:18.340
It requires grasping the entire reachability geometry of the mathematical space.

30:18.500 --> 30:20.960
It's not just knowing that A leads to B.

30:21.140 --> 30:27.300
It's knowing why A leads to B, and understanding why choosing path C would have led to a dead end.

30:27.660 --> 30:28.240
Precisely.

30:29.060 --> 30:31.900
It is possessing the mental map of the entire terrain.

30:31.900 --> 30:41.160
If an AI only operates on observational equivalents, if it is just minimizing predictive error frame by frame, it is essentially just verifying reality.

30:41.280 --> 30:44.280
It is checking the math of the universe one second at a time.

30:44.420 --> 30:50.480
But it lacks the interventional grasp, the admissibility depth to ever truly understand the world it is operating within.

30:50.600 --> 30:52.520
That is a staggering paradigm shift.

30:52.680 --> 31:00.100
It means the entire current trajectory of scaling up large language models with more passive text data isn't just hitting a point of diminishing returns.

31:00.260 --> 31:01.200
No, it's worse than that.

31:01.200 --> 31:05.820
It is mathematically incapable of crossing the threshold into true understanding.

31:06.640 --> 31:09.220
The architecture itself is the ceiling.

31:09.400 --> 31:11.220
That is the core warning of the paper.

31:11.220 --> 31:14.440
Let's hit a breath and look at the vast terrain we've covered today.

31:15.360 --> 31:21.900
We started in 1943 with Craig's dream of organisms building small-scale models of possible actions.

31:22.080 --> 31:23.160
We covered a lot of ground.

31:23.300 --> 31:24.140
We really did.

31:24.140 --> 31:36.700
We dug into the heavy, uncompromising math of the observational-interventional separation theorem, proving via Judea-Pearl's causal logic that observing a cracked beam will never teach you that it's going to break.

31:36.760 --> 31:36.980
Right.

31:36.980 --> 31:46.440
We saw how the industry's obsession with data compression literally deletes the physical realities required for safety, rendering downstream guardrails permanently blind.

31:46.680 --> 31:55.080
And finally, we saw how the geometric shape of true understanding requires us to map out reachable futures, not just predict probable observations.

31:55.080 --> 32:01.700
It forces us to re-evaluate what we are actually asking these machines to do when we hand them the keys to our physical world.

32:01.700 --> 32:03.520
But the paper doesn't stop there.

32:04.220 --> 32:13.960
In the final pages, tucked away in Appendix E, the author introduces a concept that stretches this entire framework beyond artificial intelligence and directly into the human experience.

32:14.200 --> 32:14.480
Yes.

32:14.860 --> 32:15.760
Meta-admissibility.

32:16.180 --> 32:18.960
This deals with dynamic state spaces, right?

32:19.060 --> 32:20.280
The mathematics of evolution.

32:20.660 --> 32:21.100
Exactly.

32:21.520 --> 32:29.460
We talked earlier about the cleaning robot that suddenly gets an arm and a key, its capabilities expanded, and its old map of the world became dangerously distorted.

32:29.700 --> 32:29.960
Right.

32:29.960 --> 32:39.160
But what happens when an agent, whether that's an AI or a biological human being, fundamentally and continuously evolves over the course of a lifetime?

32:39.660 --> 32:42.540
What happens when we add entirely new dimensions to our world?

32:42.540 --> 32:53.360
The paper suggests that for an identity to remain coherent across massive developmental transitions, its internal transformations must preserve fundamental reachability distinctions.

32:53.700 --> 32:57.660
The map has to update dynamically without losing the core structure of consequence.

32:57.660 --> 33:00.440
Think about a human child learning to walk.

33:00.920 --> 33:04.660
Before they can walk, the living room is mapped in a very specific way.

33:05.140 --> 33:07.120
The coffee table is a solid barrier.

33:07.620 --> 33:09.560
It is observationally an obstacle.

33:09.960 --> 33:11.620
Just like the brick wall for the robot.

33:11.880 --> 33:12.120
Yes.

33:12.540 --> 33:20.260
But the moment that child learns to pull themselves up on their own two feet, their policy class, their capabilities, radically expands.

33:20.260 --> 33:23.940
The geometry of their world permanently changes.

33:24.440 --> 33:27.200
Suddenly, the coffee table isn't a barrier anymore.

33:27.320 --> 33:28.260
It's a handhold.

33:28.680 --> 33:30.740
It is a tool for reaching the couch.

33:31.200 --> 33:33.320
The reachability set of the room has transformed.

33:34.000 --> 33:40.620
The child has to completely rewrite their internal latent space to account for this new reality.

33:40.620 --> 33:47.620
But, and this is the key of metadmissibility, as they rewrite the map, they must preserve the fundamental truths of consequence.

33:48.480 --> 33:56.700
The child must learn that the coffee table is a handhold, while simultaneously preserving the understanding that the sharp corner of the table is still a danger.

33:57.000 --> 33:57.560
Exactly.

